  "text": "The chariot is a Celtic symbol. The horse was tamed early by these Celtic peoples, among the first in Europe to do so. They did not ride the horse but hitched it to a chariot used for travel and for battle. Gods, goddesses, heroes, and heroines all appear as charioteers. The bridle and the wheel appear in the figures of divine beings such as Epona. The opposite of a curse is a charm. In Irish the charm is called eolas, a spell to draw good luck. A charm could also ward off bad luck, and one example guards a home from the evil eye. Folk used charms to speed the churning of butter, to ease the waulking of linen, and to guard the home with water.",
